Phase D — synchronous-reconcile invariant
When the action/command dispatch lands, every app_intrf handler that reaches into app_data calls app_intrf_process_data_events(app_intrf) before returning to ui_layer. That function only mutates the CX tree and appends CxEvents — never calls up into ui_layer.
Invariant: step 27 + "CX caches data_id, reconcile never re-resolves it." Under it, ui_layer may hold stale ContextIds freely (ht_get → NULL). Fixed-array storage additionally makes a borrowed pointer held across a mutating call a stale-read, not a UAF; if sampler/param storage becomes malloc'd + freed, keep that discipline strict or have ops resolve the ContextId through the module (dead → NULL) instead of storing a raw user_data.
Phase E — context → ui events
Type. typedef enum { CX_ADDED, CX_REMOVED, CX_CHANGED } CxEventType;, typedef struct { CxEventType type; ContextId id; } CxEvent;. Ring buffer in APP_INTRF.
Emit. app_intrf_cx_resync pushes CX_ADDED / CX_REMOVED; the CHANGED handler pushes CX_CHANGED.
Cursor API. bool nav_poll_event(APP_INTRF *, NavCursor *cursor, CxEvent *out) with a caller-owned NavCursor; ring wrapped past a cursor → signal "full resync".
ui_layer consumes. Each UI_STATE / view owns a NavCursor; drain per render, prune dead ContextIds from UI_TARGET_LIST etc.; on overflow rebuild that view from nav_cx_root_return down. nav_cx_is_valid stays a plain lookup.

Not really a phase — it's an invariant for later. Once there's a UI-driven mutation path (a "load plugin" action calling into app_data), every such app_intrf handler must call app_intrf_process_data_events() before returning to ui_layer, so the tree (and the CxEvents) are up to date synchronously. Today nothing wires plugin-loading to a UI action, so there's nothing to enforce yet — it's a one-line rule to apply when the action/command system lands.

Phase E — sub-phase 1: app_intrf emits CxEvents
New cx_events.h (dependency-free, like data_events.h): #include "ids.h", <stddef.h>, <stdint.h>.
typedef enum { CX_ADDED, CX_REMOVED, CX_CHANGED } CxEventType;
typedef struct { CxEventType type; ContextId id; ContextId parent; size_t index; } CxEvent;
typedef struct { uint64_t next_seq; } NavCursor;
typedef enum { NAV_POLL_EVENT, NAV_POLL_EMPTY, NAV_POLL_OVERFLOW } NavPollResult;
Ring + seq counter in APP_INTRF (app_intrf.c): struct { uint64_t seq; CxEvent ev; } cx_ring[APP_INTRF_CX_EVENT_RING] (256), uint64_t cx_seq (monotonic, last assigned; 0 = none). Zero-init in app_intrf_init.
static app_intrf_emit(APP_INTRF*, CxEventType, ContextId id, ContextId parent, size_t index) — seq = ++app_intrf->cx_seq; slot = (seq - 1) % RING; store {seq, {type,id,parent,index}}. (Overwrites the oldest silently; overflow is detected per-cursor, not here.)
Wire emits into reconcile:
cx_subtree_free → gain a bool emit param. For each node, just before app_intrf_cx_children_pop, if emit: app_intrf_emit(CX_REMOVED, cx->data_id, cx->cx_parent ? cx->cx_parent->data_id : CONTEXT_ID_NULL, cx->idx). app_intrf_cx_resync passes true; app_intrf_destroy passes false.
app_intrf_cx_resync pass 2, after app_intrf_cx_create + app_intrf_cx_children_create → app_intrf_emit(CX_ADDED, created->data_id, parent_cx->data_id, created->idx) (top node only, not the materialised subtree).
app_intrf_process_data_events DATA_EVENT_CHANGED case → app_intrf_emit(CX_CHANGED, ev.id, cx->cx_parent?…:0, cx->idx).
Public API in app_intrf.h (#include "cx_events.h"):
void nav_cursor_init(APP_INTRF*, NavCursor*) → cursor->next_seq = app_intrf->cx_seq + 1 (future only).
NavPollResult nav_poll_event(APP_INTRF*, NavCursor*, CxEvent *out):
next_seq > cx_seq → NAV_POLL_EMPTY
next_seq < oldest_live_seq (= cx_seq >= RING ? cx_seq - RING + 1 : 1) → reset next_seq = cx_seq + 1, return NAV_POLL_OVERFLOW
else copy cx_ring[(next_seq-1) % RING].ev, next_seq++, return NAV_POLL_EVENT

Checkpoint: nav_poll_event produces a stream as plugins/samples come and go; nothing consumes it; behaviour unchanged.

Phase E — sub-phase 2: ui_layer declarative stale policy
ui_layer.h: #include "app_intrf.h" (for NavCursor/CxEvent). Add
typedef enum { UI_STALE_REMOVE = 0, UI_STALE_FIRST_SIBLING, UI_STALE_NEXT_SIBLING, UI_STALE_PREV_SIBLING, UI_STALE_TO_SOURCE, UI_STALE_CLEAR } UiStaleMode;
typedef struct { UiStaleMode mode; } UiStalePolicy;
typedef enum { UI_RECONCILE_OK, UI_RECONCILE_CHANGED, UI_RECONCILE_REBUILD } UiReconcileResult;
ui_layer.c struct fields: UI_TARGET_LIST += UiStalePolicy item_policy;; UI_STATE_ENTRY += UiStalePolicy context_policy;; UI_STATE += NavCursor cursor;. All zero-init → UI_STALE_REMOVE. Init state->cursor via nav_cursor_init(ui_layer->app_intrf, &state->cursor) in ui_layer_state_init.
Policy setters:
bool ui_layer_state_entry_set_stale_policy(UI_STATE*, ContextId source, UiPurpose, UiStalePolicy);
bool ui_layer_target_list_set_stale_policy(UI_TARGET_LIST*, UiStalePolicy); (called between ..._begin / ..._end).
Raw escape-hatch helpers (for DIY policies the enum can't express):
size_t ui_layer_state_stale_targets(UI_STATE*, ContextId *out, size_t cap); — sweep occupied entries, collect ids failing nav_cx_is_valid.
bool ui_layer_target_list_remove_id(UI_TARGET_LIST*, ContextId); — compact one out.
UiReconcileResult ui_layer_state_reconcile(UI_LAYER*, UI_STATE*) — the main call:
loop nav_poll_event(ui_layer->app_intrf, &state->cursor, &ev):
NAV_POLL_OVERFLOW → best-effort full sweep-prune (via nav_cx_is_valid over all entries/items, applying policies), then return UI_RECONCILE_REBUILD.
NAV_POLL_EMPTY → break.
CX_REMOVED{id,parent,index}:
entries with context == id → apply context_policy (REMOVE entry / TO_SOURCE re-key to parent / CLEAR mark inert).
entries whose targets contain id → per occurrence apply targets.item_policy: REMOVE = ui_layer_target_list_remove_id; *_SIBLING/TO_SOURCE = query live nav_cx_children_count(parent) / nav_cx_child_at(parent, k) around index, replace the item (or replace with parent); CLEAR = empty list.
CX_CHANGED → set a per-state "redraw" flag; no structural change.
CX_ADDED → nothing (declarative vocab has no auto-follow).
return UI_RECONCILE_CHANGED if anything was touched, else UI_RECONCILE_OK.
iteration safety: re-scan the (small, open-addressed) entry table fresh per event; never hold a table cursor across a mutation.
Ordering / one-call wrapper: CxEvents are emitted inside nav_update (via app_intrf_process_data_events). Add void ui_layer_update(UI_LAYER*) = calls nav_update(app_intrf). CLI loop becomes: ui_layer_update(ui_layer) → ui_layer_state_reconcile(ui_layer, state) for each state → render.
CLI (smp_groovebox_ui_cli.c):
After ui_layer_update, call ui_layer_state_reconcile per UI_STATE; on UI_RECONCILE_REBUILD re-derive that view's parent/cursor from ui_layer_state_root_return down.
Set non-default policies where wanted, e.g. the hover/selected/current target lists → UI_STALE_PREV_SIBLING (cursor falls back to a neighbour instead of vanishing), one setter call at list creation.
helper_* functions already tolerate CONTEXT_ID_INVALID, so post-reconcile they get valid ids or empty lists — no other changes.

Checkpoint: loading/removing a plugin or sample auto-prunes every UI_STATE; target lists never accumulate dead ids; a lagging view that overflows its cursor rebuilds instead of showing stale data.
